bsorb those blunt impacts. Meanwhile, when a tiger wants to kill another tiger, it doesn’t rely on battering it with paws until it dies of internal hemorrhage; it grabs and bites the neck. That’s the equivalent of a human using a knife or chokehold, not a punch. ### Why this doesn’t “prove your point” that tigers don’t just take strikes Your original phrasing implied that since tigers generate more force, you’d expect their paw strikes to be even more lethal than human punches, yet they seem to shrug them off. My answer explained why paw strikes aren’t lethal to each other: pressure dispersion, skull/neck anatomy, different target selection, and different injury mechanisms. The fact that they **do** kill each other with bites doesn’t erase that observation—it’s just a separate, non-strike method. In a fight, they “take the strikes” (paw swats) with relatively little internal damage; it’s the follow-up bite that does the life-threatening harm. In short: **Paw strike ≠ bite.** The punch analogue is the paw strike, and that indeed rarely causes fatal internal trauma in tigers. My mention of bites wasn’t a contradiction; it was clarifying how they *actually* kill, precisely because the paw strikes alone aren’t efficient at doing so.
